## Why This Is Asked

Tests beginners learn to control external dependencies (maps, geolocation) and test isolation. This prompts demonstration of end-to-end coverage for critical user journeys while addressing flakiness through retries and data cleanup.

## Key Concepts

- Test data isolation per run
- Geolocation and permission prompts
- Network throttling and flakiness handling
- Stubbing external APIs (maps)
- Session cleanup to prevent leakage

## Code Example

```javascript
// Playwright example: mock geolocation and slow network
const context = await browser.newContext({
  geolocation: { latitude: 37.7749, longitude: -122.4194 }
});
const page = await context.newPage();
await context.overridePermissions('https://ride.example.com', ['geolocation']);
await page.route('**/maps/api/**', route => route.fulfill({ status: 200, body: JSON.stringify({ predictions: [] }) }));
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt this test to run in CI with parallel isolation?
- How would you diagnose a flaky GPS prompt in CI?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests RBAC in a realistic multi-tenant admin UI with progressive permission changes, ensuring isolation and correct UI cues.

## Key Concepts
- RBAC policies per-tenant
- Test data seeding and isolation
- UI visibility vs. API enforcement
- Role upgrade propagation without relogin

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright example sketch
test('RBAC per-tenant role upgrade', async ({ page }) => {
  await loginAsTenantUser(page, 'TenantA', 'u1', 'viewer');
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(/dashboard/);
  await expect(page.locator('#user-management')).toBeHidden();
  await upgradeRole(page, 'TenantA', 'u1', 'editor');
  await page.reload();
  await expect(page.locator('#user-management')).toBeVisible();
  await loginAsTenantUser(page, 'TenantB', 'u2', 'viewer');
  await expect(page.locator('#tenant-data')).toHaveText(/TenantA/).toBeHidden();
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle flaky network affecting role-change propagation? 
- What assertions would you add to guard against stale caches after role changes?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests for event driven checkout pipelines must prove correctness under partial failures and retries, with cross service consistency guarantees. This question probes data seeding, fault injection, idempotency, and multi-region convergence.

## Key Concepts

- Event driven end-to-end flows across microservices
- Exactly-once processing and idempotent handling
- Fault injection and chaos testing in distributed systems
- Feature flag controlled behavior and regional data isolation

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo test sketch
async function testCheckoutE2E() {
  seedRegion('us-east');
  publish('checkout', 'OrderRequested', { orderId:'ORD-1', region:'us-east' });
  enableFeatureFlag('newCheckoutFlow');
  await awaitConvergence('ORD-1', 5000);
  const ledger = readLedger('ORD-1');
  assert(ledger.status === 'COMPLETED' && ledger.total > 0);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure test flakiness and stabilize seeds?
- What metrics indicate successful convergence across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for offline-first PWAs are a common beginner challenge, ensuring service workers and syncing work under real user conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX and service worker caching
- E2E data seeding for deterministic tests
- Network control via intercepts (cy.intercept / route)
- Idempotent sync and duplicate prevention

## Code Example
```javascript
// Cypress example sketch
cy.request('POST', '/api/test/setup', {user:'test', products:[101,102]});
cy.visit('/');
cy.intercept('/api/cart', { statusCode: 503 }).as('cartOffline');
cy.get('#add-101').click();
cy.get('#add-102').click();
cy.intercept('/api/cart', (req) => req.continue()).as('cartOnline');
cy.wait('@cartOffline');
cy.get('#checkout').click();
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for a multi-user scenario with concurrent offline sessions?
- What metrics would you collect to ensure the offline sync is fast enough for a good UX?

