## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to design a realistic E2E testing scenario involving per-user feature flags, UI state validation, and data persistence. It covers test data seeding with proper user isolation, mocking external dependencies, and validating analytics signals.

## Key Concepts
- Feature flag semantics for per-user progressive rollout
- Test data seeding with user isolation
- Mocking external services
- UI assertions and persistence across reloads
- Analytics event validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Playwright E2E test
import { test, expect } from '@playwright/test';

test.describe('Notifications Center feature flag', () => {
  const enabledUser = { id: 'user-1', email: 'enabled@example.com', flags: { notificationsCenter: true } };
  const disabledUser = { id: 'user-2', email: 'disabled@example.com', flags: { notificationsCenter: false } };

  test('enabled user sees Notifications Center and receives sample notification', async ({ page }) => {
    await seedUser(enabledUser);
    await page.goto('/login');
    await page.fill('[name=email]', enabledUser.email);
    await page.fill('[name=password]', 'password123');
    await page.click('[type=submit]');

    await expect(page.locator('#notifications-panel')).toBeVisible();
    await expect(page.locator('.notification-item').first()).toContainText('Welcome to Notifications Center');
  });

  test('disabled user does not see Notifications Center', async ({ page }) => {
    await seedUser(disabledUser);
    await page.goto('/login');
    await page.fill('[name=email]', disabledUser.email);
    await page.fill('[name=password]', 'password123');
    await page.click('[type=submit]');

    await expect(page.locator('#notifications-panel')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('flag toggle persists across reload', async ({ page }) => {
    await seedUser(enabledUser);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');
    await expect(page.locator('#notifications-panel')).toBeVisible();

    await mockFlagService.setFlag(enabledUser.id, 'notificationsCenter', false);
    await page.reload();

    await expect(page.locator('#notifications-panel')).not.toBeVisible();
  });

  test('analytics events emitted correctly', async ({ page }) => {
    const analyticsEvents = [];
    page.on('request', (req) => {
      if (req.url().includes('/analytics')) analyticsEvents.push(req.postData());
    });

    await seedUser(enabledUser);
    await page.goto('/dashboard');

    expect(analyticsEvents).toContainEqual(expect.objectContaining({ event: 'notifications_panel_opened' }));
  });
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to design end-to-end tests for multi-region, multi-tenant feature flags, covering UI, API, and background workers, with rapid toggling and race conditions. Demonstrates seeds, isolation strategies, and SLA verification.

## Key Concepts

- Feature flag precedence and propagation across layers
- Cross-region consistency (UI, API, workers)
- Concurrency, load testing, and idempotency

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-test scaffold for E2E feature-flag toggles
async function testFlagToggle(tenantId, flagKey, value) {
  await api.setFlag(tenantId, flagKey, value);
  await wait(50);
  const uiState = await ui.getFlagState(tenantId, flagKey);
  const workerState = await worker.getFlagState(tenantId, flagKey);
  if (uiState !== value || workerState !== value) throw new Error('Inconsistent state');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure cross-region propagation latency? 
- How do you handle partial failures (region down) and ensure eventual consistency?
